WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.501
-Our next guest
is a very funny comedian

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.570
who kicks off her Deal With It
tour September 16th

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:06.505
at the Pantages Theatre
in Minneapolis.

00:00:06.505 --> 00:00:08.775
Please welcome to the show
Taylor Tomlinson.

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:10.276 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:13.346
[ Applause ]

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:14.380
-Thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.616
-Oh, my gosh.
Thank you for having me.

00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:19.652 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:20.754
-Thank you so much.

00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:22.421
I'm so happy to have you
here in person.

00:00:22.421 --> 00:00:24.257
And I'm also happy --
You're from California,

00:00:24.257 --> 00:00:26.926
but you now are spending time
living in New York, as well.

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:29.428
How you are finding New York?
-I am.

00:00:29.428 --> 00:00:32.598
You know, I miss my car a lot.

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:33.900
I'm used to driving --

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.736
Like, I miss crying in a car
that I'm driving.

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:38.571
-Sure.
-Like, here can you do it,

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:40.773
but you're like, "Oh, my God.
I'm not in control of my life

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:42.775
or even this vehicle."
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:45.144
-It's very triggering.
But, yeah, my boyfriend

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:49.415
lives in New York and is, like,
born and raised in Manhattan,

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:51.651
which I consider
low-key child abuse, but...

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:53.086
[ Laughter ]

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:54.687
...he's like, "If I have kids,
we have to raise them here,"

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.589
and I was like,
"I'm gonna love my children."

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:58.258
[ Laughter ]

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:02.462
But I've learned that, like,
when you are single,

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.363
and you're looking for somebody,
like, you need to make a list

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:06.665
of all the things you want
in a partner, right?

00:01:06.665 --> 00:01:08.401
'Cause if you forget
to put something on there,

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.503
the universe is like, "Well,
technically, you didn't say,

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.838
so we didn't bring you that."

00:01:12.838 --> 00:01:17.410
And because he grew up
in New York, he cannot drive.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.045
Didn't even know
I had to ask for that.

00:01:20.045 --> 00:01:23.249
Thought that kind of just
came with the fully grown man.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.251
[ Laughter ]
And let me be clear.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:27.853
He could drive, but he won't.
-Oh.

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:31.023
-He, like, won't drive, because
he's like, "I'm a New Yorker."

00:01:31.023 --> 00:01:33.326
Like, you know what I mean?
-Yeah, I do.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:34.727
-I'm like,
"What are you gonna do

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.229
if there's, like,
an apocalypse?"

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:39.265
And he's like, "There are Lyfts,
there are Ubers."

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:43.336
I'm like, "What do you think
Uber drivers are gonna be doing

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:45.471
during the apocalypse?
You think they're gonna be

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:49.108
hopping on the app, like,
'Oh, hell yeah, surge pricing.

00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:53.179
Must be 'cause Satan's here.'"
Like, it's just crazy to me.

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:57.417
-You -- when I saw
Quarter-Life Crisis,

00:01:57.417 --> 00:02:00.219
your special on Netflix --
And I feel this is something

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:01.553
that is true of me
as I get older.

00:02:01.553 --> 00:02:04.257
I become very resentful
of young talented people.

00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:07.227
And I was like, "How is she
so good, so young?"

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:08.661
And then I felt
a little bit better,

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:13.199
because you started very young
doing stand-up in churches.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:15.101
-Yeah.
Yeah, I started when I was 16.

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:17.270
That's the only place
you can do stand-up

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:19.739
when you're 16 is in church.
-Uh-huh.

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:22.207
-And only if you do
an altar call at the end.

00:02:22.207 --> 00:02:24.944
If you end it with like,
"And Jesus died for your sins,"

00:02:24.944 --> 00:02:27.580
then they're like,
"Okay. You can do 15.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.381
That's fine."
But, like, comedy clubs

00:02:29.381 --> 00:02:31.451
won't let you perform
until you're 18.

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:33.186
-Gotcha.
-And even then,

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:35.387
it's a little sketchy.
-And so what --

00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:37.290
And did you find --

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.793
Did the material go over well
with your church crowd?

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:44.130
-You know, I spent most
of the first couple years

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.766
of my stand-up "career"...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.766 --> 00:02:51.437
...being, like, very apologetic
for just being so young.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:54.974
Like, the first joke I ever did,
how I used to open sets,

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:57.176
is I used to say, like, "I know
there's probably gonna be

00:02:57.176 --> 00:02:59.979
like a generational gap here.
You know, I'm so young.

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:03.282
Like, you guys saw 'Titanic'
in theaters, I saw it on VHS.

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:05.584
Like, you cried
because Jack died.

00:03:05.584 --> 00:03:07.987
I cried because
Leonardo DiCaprio

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:11.591
didn't look like that anymore."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:14.126
-Would -- so Deal With It
is the new special.

00:03:14.126 --> 00:03:16.028
Why are you calling --

00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:17.597
The new hour you're touring
with, I should say.

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:21.967
Why is it called Deal With It?
-The new hour that I'm doing now

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:24.970
is a bit darker than
Quarter-Life Crisis was.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:26.906
Like, Quarter-Life Crisis,
my Netflix special,

00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:29.108
is a lot about, like,
being in your 20s

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:31.277
and, like, it sucks
and it's hard

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:34.447
'cause you're in transition.
And this new hour

00:03:34.447 --> 00:03:37.683
is, like, getting to the bottom
of why you suck.

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:41.320
Like, "Okay. We know we suck.
Let's figure out what it is,"

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:45.090
with, like, a lot of therapy
and soul searching and whatnot.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:48.561
So I named the tour Deal With It
because it's, like, dealing

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:50.729
with your issues, dealing
with your childhood trauma.

00:03:50.729 --> 00:03:53.099
And also, if you saw
Quarter-Life Crisis

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:54.467
and you come to see me,
and you're like,

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:56.902
"Why aren't there more
dating jokes?"

00:03:56.902 --> 00:03:58.104
Deal with it.
This is the show.

00:03:58.104 --> 00:04:00.573
-Right.
-This is the show.

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:03.509
-You actually talk about
and went through

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:05.811
what a lot of people did,
which is continuing

00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:07.413
to do therapy during a pandemic
where you couldn't

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:09.982
be in the room with someone,
and having to do it over Zoom.

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:11.917
-Mm-hmm.
-How did you find that?

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:15.287
-Well, I started seeing a new
therapist during the pandemic.

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:17.389
My old one,
in one of our last sessions,

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:19.158
told me that
when I get close to people,

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:20.993
I have a tendency
to self-sabotage,

00:04:20.993 --> 00:04:23.329
and, like, I thought that was
bull [bleep] so I ghosted her.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:24.830
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:29.202
And I started seeing my current
therapist during the pandemic.

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:31.904
So I only recently
met her in person.

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:32.972
And, like, oh, man --

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:34.273
Have you done Zoom therapy,
Seth?

00:04:34.273 --> 00:04:35.574
-I haven't done Zoom therapy.

00:04:35.574 --> 00:04:38.477
-Oh, it's so much fun
watching your trauma buffer.

00:04:38.477 --> 00:04:42.648
It's a great time.
Watching your issues

00:04:42.648 --> 00:04:46.318
struggle under the weight
of your weak Wi-Fi.

00:04:46.318 --> 00:04:48.488
It's really -- it's great.
Every week, I was like,

00:04:48.488 --> 00:04:50.155
"It's just hard.
My dad doesn't listen."

00:04:50.155 --> 00:04:53.425
She's like, "I can't hear you.
You froze.

00:04:53.425 --> 00:04:55.327
If you're still talking,
I'm not getting any of it.

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:57.496
Luckily, I have
your credit-card information.

00:04:57.496 --> 00:04:59.264
I'll see you next week."

00:04:59.264 --> 00:05:01.800
-You -- we were talking
backstage about the timing.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.706
I felt deeply empathetic
for comedians who maybe --

00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:09.141
the shutdown happened right
before they were about to tape.

00:05:09.141 --> 00:05:10.609
Like, that's the worst, right?

00:05:10.609 --> 00:05:13.146
You have an hour ready to go,
and you have to wait so long.

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:15.615
Your special came out
right before.

00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:17.583
And somebody might think,
"Oh, that's bad timing,"

00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:20.887
'cause a big special comes out,
you can then go sell tickets.

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:23.322
But it actually turned out
to be pretty good timing.

00:05:23.322 --> 00:05:25.458
-Yeah, I mean,
I was very nervous,

00:05:25.458 --> 00:05:28.861
because it came out
the week before lockdown.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:32.298
So just under the wire.
And I was thinking, like,

00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:35.067
"Okay. Well, I guess
I missed my moment here."

00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:37.270
Like, by the time everything
goes back to normal,

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:40.540
if it ever does, I --
people will have forgotten

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:42.574
that this special even came out.

00:05:42.574 --> 00:05:44.476
Meanwhile, my agents
are calling me like,

00:05:44.476 --> 00:05:48.180
"This is a great time
to have a special come out."

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.282
Like, seriously, people
are trapped in their homes.

00:05:50.282 --> 00:05:53.352
They're watching stuff
they wouldn't even consider

00:05:53.352 --> 00:05:55.455
watching if they
could go outside.

00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:58.958
Like, "People are really giving
stuff a chance right now."

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:01.460
And I'm like, "Okay.
I would have hoped this did well

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.962
without a global emergency,
but I'll take it."

00:06:03.962 --> 00:06:06.498
-You saying that makes me
realize how many agents

00:06:06.498 --> 00:06:09.134
probably told clients, "You are
crushing this pandemic."

00:06:09.134 --> 00:06:12.204
-Oh, my God. This one --
"COVID's been great for you."

00:06:12.204 --> 00:06:15.775
[ Laughter ]
I heard that so many times.

00:06:15.775 --> 00:06:18.410
-I think it's very exciting,
as we do come out of it,

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:21.013
hopefully, that people
will be able to go

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:23.216
and see you perform again.
And you've got a lot

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:24.583
of great tour dates
in a lot of great cities.

00:06:24.583 --> 00:06:25.684
And thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:25.684 --> 00:06:26.752
It's really lovely
talking to you.

00:06:26.752 --> 00:06:27.854
-Thank you so much
for having me.

00:06:27.854 --> 00:06:29.088
-Taylor's Deal With It tour

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:30.723
starts September 16th
in Minneapolis.

